Page 17287 estar: Found match for regex: =
Page 17287 estar: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{inh+|ca|la|stāre||stand}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|stō||stand}}. Compare {{cog|oc|estar}} and {{cog|fro|ester}}. Where the velar infix that is characteristic for the second conjugation comes from is unknown.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ca-IPA}}
* {{audio|ca|LL-Q7026 (cat)-Unjoanqualsevol-estar.wav|Audio}}
* {{rhymes|ca|a(ɾ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{ca-verb|pres_1_sg=estic}}

# {{lb|ca|transitive|copular}} to [[be]]; to [[currently]] [[be]] in a [[state]] or have a [[characteristic]] {{gloss|Used to connect a noun to an adjective that describes a temporary state of being.}}
#: {{ux|ca|'''Estic''' cansada.|I '''am''' tired.|inline=1}}
# {{lb|ca|auxiliary}} {{non-gloss definition|forms the [[continuous]] [[aspect]], together with a [[present participle]]}}
#: {{ux|ca|Ja '''està''' dormint.|He '''is''' already sleeping.|inline=1}}
# {{lb|ca|intransitive|+adverbial phrase}} to be [[located]] {{gloss|to be in a place}}
#: {{ux|ca|La Torre Eiffel '''està''' a París.|The Eiffel Tower '''is''' in Paris.|inline=1}}

====Usage notes====
This is one of two verbs that can be translated as ''to be'', the other being {{m|ca|ser}}/{{m|ca|ésser}}. ''Ser/ésser'' indicates something that is inherent and not expected to change, whereas {{m|ca|estar}} describes temporary qualities that apply only at a particular time. ''Ser/ésser'' relates to {{m|ca|estar}} as {{m|en|essence}} relates to {{m|en|state}}, etymologically as well as semantically.

====Conjugation====
{{ca-conj-estar}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ca|benestar}}
* {{l|ca|estada}}
* {{l|ca|estament}}
* {{l|ca|estança}}
* {{l|ca|malestar}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|ca|estadant}}
* {{l|ca|estar-se}}
* {{l|ca|estaria}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:IEC2}}
* {{R:GDLC}}
* {{R:DNV}}
* {{R:DCVB}}

-------- end text --------
